# Firebase and persisted data

- Firebase Authentication UID is the user identity and user document ID.
- Keep Firebase operations behind the existing repository/Firestore adapter boundaries for new code.
- Preserve snake_case fields used by existing documents: examples include `full_name`, `user_id`, `movement_name`, `duration_seconds`, `feedback_type`, and `created_at`.
- Treat server timestamps as nullable or pending when reading immediately after writes.
- A model change must consider Dart models, Firestore mapping, repository queries, security rules, indexes, existing documents, and README/API documentation.
- A new compound query must be reconciled with `firestore.indexes.json`.
- UI visibility is not authorization. Keep ownership checks in `firestore.rules`.
- Never store passwords, auth tokens, service-account keys, raw frames, or unnecessary sensitive data in Firestore.
- Do not deploy Firestore rules/indexes without explicit user intent and human review.
- Do not point the app at a different Firebase project casually; generated options and project files must remain consistent.
- Preserve session/feedback linkage and user ownership when changing write flows.

## Leaderboard (Firestore)

- `leaderboard/{userId}` uses the Firebase UID as document ID and `user_id`.
- `leaderboard_processed_sessions/{sessionId}` is the duplicate-award idempotency marker.
- Leaderboard awards must be idempotent; do not award the same session twice.
- The source `sessions/{sessionId}` document must exist and belong to the authenticated user.
- The processed marker must match the session, user, score, and `xp_awarded` (25) according to `firestore.rules`.
- Keep aggregate fields synchronized with `LeaderboardEntry`, `LeaderboardAwardPlan`, `LeaderboardRepository`, and `firestore.rules`: `display_name`, `total_xp`, `sessions_completed`, `score_sum`, `average_score`, `best_score`, `last_session_at`, `updated_at`, `last_awarded_session_id`.
- Keep leaderboard query ordering synchronized with `firestore.indexes.json` (`total_xp` descending, then `best_score` descending).
- Preserve authenticated leaderboard reads and own-document write constraints; do not weaken marker update/delete restrictions.
- Display-name-only updates must not alter XP or score aggregates.
- The current client transaction model is suitable for the controlled capstone environment but must not be described as server-authoritative or hostile-client-proof.
- Camera settings are stored locally and must not be described as Firestore data.

For data changes, verify mapping round trips, null timestamps, empty data, permission behavior, and query/index alignment.
